"An earlier lesson in this unit introduced the eight extraordinary vessels as the meridian system's reservoirs, distinct from the twelve main meridians tied to specific organs. Two of those eight have become far more widely known outside Chinese medicine than the rest, largely through martial-arts fiction: the governing vessel and the conception vessel, running respectively along the midline of the back and the midline of the front of the body. In popular fiction, \"breaking through\" these two vessels is often portrayed as a dramatic, almost magical breakthrough unlocking hidden power. The traditional medical description is considerably less dramatic: the governing vessel is said to govern all yang meridians, acting as a kind of collecting point for yang qi running along the back, while the conception vessel plays the equivalent role for yin, collecting yin qi running along the front — together forming a front-back axis that the other meridians are traditionally described as connecting into. Because they aren't tied to a single organ the way the twelve main meridians are, the governing and conception vessels are traditionally treated less as an emergency reserve for a single crisis and more as a structural spine the rest of the system leans on — one more reason overall posture and spinal alignment are traditionally discussed alongside meridian health, rather than in a separate category of \"just movement.\" A fictional shortcut to power turns out, in the original framework, to be a description of structural support running quietly along two straight lines.": "811bb795a603",
